    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d100">

                <pb facs="0154" n="104" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg104"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Denby, chargé, to
                        Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-08-21"><hi rendition="#italic">August 21,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I have received your cipher telegram. According to the Yamên statement, prefect
                    of Shanghai on the 13th saw in the French concession two Japanese wearing
                    Chinese clothing, and securing arrest by the French consul, plans were found
                    upon them. French consul delivered them to the consul general of the United
                    States, who refused to give them up without definite instructions of legation of
                    the United States. Yamên requested their delivery. I replied I could not act
                    until the U. S. consul-general has reported. The U. S. consul-general telegraphs
                    accused asked for asylum until the case investigated. Was granted with this
                    understanding, that <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> shall be maintained.
                    Accused papers safe. Important principle involved. The rights of China doubted.
                    The U. S. consul-general urges the legation to await written report, expected to
                    arrive to-morrow. I have assured Yamên of impartiality and request delay. On
                    receiving report of U. S. consul-general will telegraph.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
